#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/id #uuid "adb10509-7cc7-58fb-9c2c-5a219d0bec86" :question/math-academy-id "e-12154" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=5^{2x}-20$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 1.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (5^{2x}-20)' = 2\\ln (5)\\cdot 5^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{5}^{2{x}_{n}}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{5}^{2{x}_{0}}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2{x}_{0}}}. \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2}}. \\\\  & ≈0.938,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092371 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/id #uuid "35f589f7-0823-50bf-9bee-e2b9202bfda3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12155" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=e^x-x^3-3$ in the interval $[4,6].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (e^x-x^3-3)' = e^x-3x^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-{x}_{n}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-{x}_{0}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{{e}^{5}-{5}^{3}-3}{{e}^{5}-3(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =4.721\\,941…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{1}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =(4.721\\,941…)-\\frac{{e}^{4.721\\,941…}-(4.721\\,941…)^{3}-3}{{e}^{4.721\\,941…}-3(4.721\\,941…)^{2}} \\\\  & ≈4.632,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "af6352ce-9fb6-5951-962a-8fe316f05c50" :question/math-academy-id "e-12156" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x^5-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (x^5-2)' = 5x^4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{5}-2}{5{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(5{x}_{n}^{4})-({x}_{n}^{5}-2)}{5{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{n}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1)^{5}+2}{5(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{4{x}_{1}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{1}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.2)^{5}+2}{5(1.2)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.152… \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{4{x}_{2}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.152…)^{5}+2}{5(1.152…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.148….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx 1.15$ and $x_3 \\approx 1.15$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x \\approx 1.15,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092371 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c12c039a-544c-53ae-a905-9273d45e6e2b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090213}] :question/id #uuid "d0352844-0411-514a-99d2-799ae24bfc7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-255771" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = x^{3} - x - 1$ in the interval $[1, 2]$. Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 1$. Round your answer to three decimal places.$x_{2} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092371 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a99190-855b-5a97-9d34-03834cbd05d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20a99190-855b-5a97-9d34-03834cbd05d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290770} :answer-field/id #uuid "0860cd97-7df0-517b-85eb-0477ff1f4fa0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a1bcff-e6be-5276-bd40-a5e193187ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a1bcff-e6be-5276-bd40-a5e193187ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291417} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9197374-8ffc-5179-b8a7-723e02b609fd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55659377-1a68-5cde-bfb5-257e09e1e167" :question/math-academy-id "q-255772" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-1}{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}})} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-({x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0} \\\\  & =2(1.5)-(1.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5) \\\\  & =2.391\\,802….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2(2.391\\,802…)-(2.391\\,802…)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2.391\\,802…) \\\\  & =2.697\\,840….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.392$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.698,$ rounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb6f9700-5c49-58d9-b28f-a243f935ab8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb6f9700-5c49-58d9-b28f-a243f935ab8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291699} :answer-field/id #uuid "17965307-e20c-593e-b88e-18778fab1bcf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d82ea011-ddd7-5eaf-8739-fbc1e56a9b6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-255829" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-4{x}^{2}$ has a root in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Netwon-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to one decimal place.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}-4{x}^{2})^{′}={e}^{x}-8x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-4{x}_{n}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-8{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}^{{x}_{n}}-8{x}_{n})-({e}^{{x}_{n}}-4{x}_{n}^{2})}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-8{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}-4{x}_{n}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-8{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}-4{x}_{0}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-8{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1{e}^{1}-{e}^{1}-4(1)^{2}}{{e}^{1}-8(1)} \\\\  & =0.757….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}-4{x}_{1}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-8{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.757…){e}^{0.757…}-{e}^{0.757…}-4(0.757…)^{2}}{{e}^{0.757…}-8(0.757…)} \\\\  & =0.716…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{2}}-{e}^{{x}_{2}}-4{x}_{2}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-8{x}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.716…){e}^{0.716…}-{e}^{0.716…}-4(0.716…)^{2}}{{e}^{0.716…}-8(0.716…)} \\\\  & =0.714….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.7$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.7$ are the same when rounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈0.7,$ rounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a446cdb3-c0af-504d-9a54-bb2be0c3ba25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a446cdb3-c0af-504d-9a54-bb2be0c3ba25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291350} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbe60cfe-5f51-58fc-bd9d-0958798e7eaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b69ecb8-18be-5795-8ca2-bd6abf263b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-256466" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=-1.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n})-({x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+3{x}_{0}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1.2)^{3}+3(-1.2)^{2}+2}{3(-1.2)^{2}+6(-1.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.864}{(-2.88)} \\\\  & ≈-0.994,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092371 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89e7a585-14e6-5a0b-bc32-c8f1dd21204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.941" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89e7a585-14e6-5a0b-bc32-c8f1dd21204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.941" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291216} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d68ae9-685b-5ad4-98df-1a962852f2a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e48e9bd-4f64-588a-8bbc-610b3e1586aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-256499" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}+8$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}+8)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-20x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}+8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n})-({x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}+8)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}-10{x}_{0}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{0}^{3}-20{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(0.5)^{4}-10(0.5)^{2}-8}{4(0.5)^{3}-20(0.5)} \\\\  & =1.085\\,526…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.085\\,526…)^{4}-10(1.085\\,526…)^{2}-8}{4(1.085\\,526…)^{3}-20(1.085\\,526…)} \\\\  & ≈0.941,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092371 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ea3be7-63fd-5d38-a6c4-84e914a0d57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.396" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47ea3be7-63fd-5d38-a6c4-84e914a0d57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.396" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290926} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ce7f63-8273-54a2-a91c-c4fbe41262b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c4fa760-3256-5c22-b663-79a18f288418" :question/math-academy-id "q-256510" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2x-1$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-2x-1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}^{4}-2x-1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-2)-({x}_{n}^{4}-2{x}_{n}-1)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{0}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.5)^{4}+1}{4(1.5)^{3}-2} \\\\  & =1.407\\,608…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{4}-1}{4{x}_{1}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.407\\,608…)^{4}-1}{4(1.407\\,608…)^{3}-2} \\\\  & ≈1.396,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "018ebb62-d5c7-50c4-9ff2-382590183f73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090218}] :question/id #uuid "760a43e9-b1f9-58a7-8f62-ff75f510cddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-256514" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x) = \\sin ((x - 4)^{2}) - (x - 6)$ in the interval $[5, 6]$. Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_{0} = 5.5$. Round your answer to three decimal places.$x_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f15b9778-7eeb-5057-9816-366cdf07f7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f15b9778-7eeb-5057-9816-366cdf07f7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291644} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2211671-c87b-5af3-925c-c70df0bb7ccf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "013fb993-c34e-58d5-ab5a-96a9dfc2d8f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-256582" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+4x-6$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.3$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+4x-6)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+4)-({x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.3)^{3}+6}{3(1.3)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.145…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{1}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.145…)^{3}+6}{3(1.145…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{2}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.134…)^{3}+6}{3(1.134…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.13$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.13$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.13,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe6b6423-280a-518a-a37b-7087368eaf40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe6b6423-280a-518a-a37b-7087368eaf40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291718} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8196514-e01e-53a8-87c9-b13f0f9aa45e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09d5d54f-fddc-5f68-b1ba-4119ada7542d" :question/math-academy-id "q-256588" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-x-1$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}-x-1)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)-({x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.5)^{3}+1}{3(1.5)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.347….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{1}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.347…)^{3}+1}{3(1.347…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.325…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.325…)^{3}+1}{3(1.325…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{2{x}_{3}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{3}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.324…)^{3}+1}{3(1.324…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.32$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.32$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.32,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0978732-5f0e-5282-9335-162d3c037a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0978732-5f0e-5282-9335-162d3c037a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291399} :answer-field/id #uuid "54c026e3-b2cd-5614-8267-7aebb7db7238" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce1e65e3-3237-5496-b03f-b34039bbc935" :question/math-academy-id "q-256604" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-x-4$ has a root in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to one decimal place.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}-x-4)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}-x-4}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)-({x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-4)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+4}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+4}{3{x}_{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.5)^{3}+4}{3(1.5)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.869…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+4}{3{x}_{1}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.869…)^{3}+4}{3(1.869…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.799…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+4}{3{x}_{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.799…)^{3}+4}{3(1.799…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.796….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.8$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.8$ are the same when rounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.8,$ rounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e98b48b-e09a-524c-a1fd-202536bbdb0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e98b48b-e09a-524c-a1fd-202536bbdb0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290968} :answer-field/id #uuid "d970493d-b5a8-5591-ae32-1929a0baff29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79ea7f86-725b-5ac1-990c-02d59dacbbed" :question/math-academy-id "q-256612" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-8x-3$ has a root in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=2$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-8x-3)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-8)-({x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}_{n}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{n}^{3}-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3({x}_{0}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{0}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({2}^{4}+1)}{4({2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{24} \\\\  & =2.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3({x}_{1}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{1}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.125)^{4}+1)}{4((2.125)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.112…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3({x}_{2}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.112…)^{4}+1)}{4((2.112…)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.111….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈2.11$ and ${x}_{3}≈2.11$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈2.11,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "677b96e3-3b61-5831-9d11-3e6014950474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.355" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "677b96e3-3b61-5831-9d11-3e6014950474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.355" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291072} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e50f30-5144-5fc9-bbe4-2c993c917c8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31695794-a02a-5139-9ef2-dcc26171634b" :question/math-academy-id "q-256623" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=-{e}^{-x}+3{x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+{x}_{n}^{3}-2{x}_{n}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+{x}_{0}^{3}-2{x}_{0}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-2} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{-0.5}+{0.5}^{3}-2\\cdot 0.5}{-{e}^{-0.5}+3\\cdot {0.5}^{2}-2} \\\\  & ≈0.355,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}]}
